.TH ZERCH 1 "2026-09-18" "zerch 0.7.5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zerch \- a less-but-smarter terminal log viewer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zerch
.I logfile
.RB [ \-\-view
.IR string ]
.RB [ \-\-fresh ]
.RB [ \-\-web ]
.RB [ \-\-web\-port
.IR N ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zerch
is a terminal log viewer with search, filters, highlights, bookmarks, and
log\-level / timestamp awareness. It builds an in\-memory index (memory\-mapping
the file and scanning it in parallel), so it opens multi\-gigabyte files nearly
instantly and searches them in well under a second.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-view " string"
Restore a saved view: filters, highlights, bookmarks, revealed ranges, the
search term, and the selected line, encoded as a single compact, shareable
token \(em so you can save or hand off an exact view. Takes precedence over a
saved session.
.TP
.B \-\-fresh
Open the file clean, ignoring its saved session (see
.BR SESSIONS ).
.TP
.B \-\-web
Open the log in a local browser tab instead of the terminal UI, reusing the same
engine and view model (see
.BR "WEB UI" ).
.TP
.BI \-\-web\-port " N"
Bind the
.B \-\-web
server to port
.IR N
(implies
.BR \-\-web ).
The default is an ephemeral port.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.SH KEYS
Press
.B ?
inside the viewer for the full, interactive cheat\-sheet. A summary:
.TP
.B j k \(ua \(da f b g G
Move by line, page, or to the top/bottom.
.TP
.B H M L
Move the cursor to the top, middle, or bottom of the visible screen (no scroll).
.TP
.BI : N
Go to line
.IR N ;
.B :$
goes to the last line;
.B :help
opens the cheat\-sheet;
.B :q
quits. The
.B :
prompt keeps its own history \(em press
.B \(ua \(da
to recall recent commands.
.TP
.B \(<- \(-> 0 w
Pan long lines (half a screen), reset pan, toggle in\-place wrap. A numeric
prefix pans by that many columns, e.g.
.B 1 \(->
shifts one column right.
.TP
.B / ?
Search forward / backward, starting from the cursor and wrapping (a vim\-style
notice appears when a search wraps past an end of the file);
.B n N
repeat in the search direction / reverse it;
.B ^R
regex,
.B ^A
case,
.B \(ua \(da
recall recent inputs.
.TP
.B F
Add a filter (reduces the view to matching lines);
.B o
toggle AND/OR.
.TP
.B h
Add a coloured highlight. Filter and highlight prompts also support
.BR ^R / ^A .
.TP
.B B [ ]
Toggle a bookmark; jump to the previous/next bookmark.
.TP
.B x
Hide the cursor's line; it collapses into a gap. Reveal the gap (see
.BR Enter )
or press
.B X
in the menu to clear all hidden lines.
.TP
.B Enter
On a line: open a JSON view, or a wrapped view if it does not fit. On a gap:
reveal hidden lines.
.TP
.B T t
Show/hide the time histogram and enter the time\-brush.
.TP
.B m
Menu: edit/delete filters and highlights, export the view string or an
equivalent grep/awk command. It also has inline-editable
.BR Separator ,
.BR "Hide Columns" ,
and
.B Bookmarks
fields (press Enter to edit). The listed 1\-based columns, split on the
separator, are replaced with an ellipsis in the view; the bookmarks field is a
comma-separated list of line numbers. Both the Hide Columns and Bookmarks
lists accept inclusive ranges, so
.I 1,3\-5,8
is the same as
.IR 1,3,4,5,8 .
Filter and highlight terms share one colour palette (each shown as a swatch in
the menu) and their matches are boxed in that colour in the view;
.B l
toggles filter colouring,
.B s
toggles syntax colouring, and
.B z
toggles zebra striping (a faint tint on alternate lines). These display
toggles are remembered per file across sessions.
.TP
.B q ^C
Quit (press
.B q
twice, or
.BR ^C ,
to exit immediately).
.SH WEB UI
With
.BR \-\-web ,
zerch serves the log to a local browser tab over loopback\-only HTTP instead of
drawing the terminal UI. It offers clickable filters (with per\-term counts),
highlights, log\-level chips, live search with n/N navigation, bookmarks and
revealed ranges, hide/reveal, a minimap, a drag\-to\-filter time histogram,
per\-line JSON pretty\-print and wrap, and a grep/awk export, reusing the same
engine and view model. The entire view is encoded in the page URL as the same
.B \-\-view
token, so every view is a shareable link and each browser tab is independent.
The server binds
.B 127.0.0.1
only, prints its URL, and attempts to open the browser
.RB ( open ", " xdg\-open ", or " "cmd /c start" );
if no opener is found the URL is still printed. Stop it with Ctrl\-C.
.PP
.B \-\-web
serves over plain HTTP with no authentication: any local process or user
that can reach the port can read the log. It is intended for a single\-user
local machine, not a shared host.
.SH SESSIONS
Each file's view \(em filters, highlights, bookmarks, hidden lines, search, and
the cursor line \(em is saved on exit and restored on the next
.BI "zerch " logfile ,
keyed by the file's canonical path, so you resume where you left off. Sessions
live under
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/zerch/sessions/
(private, 0600). Use
.B \-\-fresh
to skip restoring once, or set
.B ZERCH_NO_SESSION=1
to disable sessions entirely. Piped input has no session.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ZERCH_NAIVE_TZ
Interpretation for offset\-less timestamps:
.BR utc ", " local ", or a fixed offset like " +02:00 .
.TP
.B ZERCH_HISTFILE
Path to the persistent input\-history file (the last 100 search / filter /
highlight terms, recalled with the up and down arrows). Defaults to
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/zerch/history
(or
.IR ~/.local/state/zerch/history ).
Set to
.B \-
or empty to disable history persistence. The file is written owner\-readable
only (0600).
.TP
.B ZERCH_NO_SESSION
When set to a non\-empty value (other than
.BR 0 ),
disables per\-file session saving and restoring.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/zerch/history
Persistent input history (unless overridden by
.BR ZERCH_HISTFILE ).
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/zerch/sessions/
Per\-file saved views (one file per log, named by a hash of its path).
